window.SMARTCOM_READING_ARTICLES=[{"id":"earthquake","category":"Earth Science","icon":"🌍","title":"Why Earthquakes Happen","level":"B1–B2","source":"U.S. Geological Survey (USGS)","sourceTitle":"What is an earthquake and what causes them to happen?","sourceUrl":"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-earthquake-and-what-causes-them-happen","license":"USGS-authored U.S. public-domain information; Smartcom educational adaptation","paragraphs":["Earthquakes begin far beneath our feet. The rocky outer layer of Earth is divided into large tectonic plates that move slowly over time. Their boundaries are not always smooth. Along a fault, rocks on opposite sides can become locked together even while the plates continue to move. This creates stress inside the surrounding rock.","Eventually, the accumulated stress may become greater than the force holding the rocks in place. The fault slips suddenly, releasing energy. This energy moves through the ground as seismic waves. People at the surface feel the movement as an earthquake. Some earthquakes are so weak that they are detected mainly by instruments, while others cause dangerous shaking.","California provides a well-known example. The San Andreas Fault marks a major boundary between the Pacific Plate and the North American Plate. Movement along this fault system is measured over many years, but it does not happen in exactly the same way everywhere. Some sections move gradually; other sections can remain locked for long periods. Studying these differences helps scientists understand earthquake hazards.","Although scientists monitor faults and record seismic activity, an exact prediction of the day and time of a future major earthquake remains beyond current capabilities. This is why preparation, safer buildings and clear emergency plans are important."],"topic":"science","originalText":false},{"id":"interior","category":"Earth Science","icon":"🗺️","title":"Earthquakes Far from Plate Boundaries","level":"B2","source":"U.S. Geological Survey (USGS)","sourceTitle":"Why do earthquakes occur in the middle of the continent far from plate boundaries?","sourceUrl":"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/why-do-earthquakes-occur-middle-continent-far-plate-boundaries","license":"USGS-authored U.S. public-domain information; Smartcom educational adaptation","paragraphs":["Most people associate earthquakes with the edges of tectonic plates. These are areas where enormous pieces of Earth's crust collide, separate or move past one another. However, earthquakes are not limited to plate boundaries. They can also occur deep within continental interiors, far from the most familiar fault lines.","Scientists believe that the history of a region can influence its present-day earthquake activity. Long ago, a continent may have experienced mountain-building, stretching or other geological processes. These events left zones of weakness in the rock, including ancient faults and fractures. Even when the landscape appears quiet today, those weaknesses may still exist beneath the surface.","Stress from moving plate boundaries can travel through a large area of crust. When that stress reaches an old fault, it may cause the rocks to slip again. Because such events are relatively uncommon in the continental interior, they are difficult to study and predict.","This research highlights an important lesson: a place does not have to stand directly on a major plate boundary to experience seismic risk. Scientists compare geological records, modern measurements and evidence from past earthquakes to understand how these less obvious hazards develop."],"topic":"science","originalText":false},{"id":"volcano","category":"Natural Hazards","icon":"🌋","title":"How Scientists Listen to Volcanoes","level":"B1–B2","source":"U.S. Geological Survey (USGS)","sourceTitle":"Monitoring Volcano Seismicity Provides Insight to Volcanic Structure","sourceUrl":"https://www.usgs.gov/programs/VHP/monitoring-volcano-seismicity-provides-insight-volcanic-structure","license":"USGS-authored U.S. public-domain information; Smartcom educational adaptation","paragraphs":["A volcano may look calm from a distance, but activity below the surface can tell a different story. Scientists study earthquakes near volcanoes because moving magma and gases may place stress on surrounding rock. Small fractures can produce seismic signals that sensitive instruments record.","Not every volcanic earthquake signals an approaching eruption. Volcanoes experience many kinds of seismic activity, and some events happen without leading to an eruption at all. Researchers therefore look for patterns rather than relying on a single measurement.","Seismologists compare different signals, including ordinary fault-related earthquakes, low-frequency events and prolonged shaking called tremor. Each type can provide clues about processes underground. However, the interpretation is not always simple. The same volcano may behave differently at different times.","Earthquake monitoring is most useful when combined with other observations, such as gas emissions, changes in the ground surface and temperature. Together these measurements help scientists recognize unusual activity and communicate potential hazards to nearby communities. Monitoring reduces uncertainty, but it cannot remove every risk."],"topic":"science","originalText":false},{"id":"subduction","category":"Natural Hazards","icon":"🌊","title":"What Happens at a Subduction Zone?","level":"B2","source":"U.S. Geological Survey (USGS)","sourceTitle":"Introduction to Subduction Zones: Amazing Events in Subduction Zones","sourceUrl":"https://www.usgs.gov/special-topics/subduction-zone-science/science/introduction-subduction-zones-amazing-events","license":"USGS-authored U.S. public-domain information; Smartcom educational adaptation","paragraphs":["At a subduction zone, one tectonic plate moves beneath another and descends into Earth's interior. This slow process takes place over enormous distances and can affect both the shape of the seafloor and the land above it. Some of the world's most powerful earthquakes occur in these regions.","The boundary between two plates does not always slide smoothly. Parts of it may become locked while the plates continue to push against each other. Over time, stress builds until a sudden movement releases it. When a very large area moves, the earthquake can be exceptionally powerful.","Under certain conditions, movement of the seafloor can push large amounts of water and generate a tsunami. Subduction zones are also associated with volcanic activity and changes in the landscape. These hazards are connected to the same broad tectonic processes, although each event develops differently.","Researchers use geological surveys, ocean measurements and seismic instruments to study these complex regions. Their findings support hazard maps, coastal planning and public education. Understanding subduction is therefore not only a matter of scientific curiosity; it can help communities prepare for serious natural events."],"topic":"science","originalText":false},{"id":"groundwater","category":"Water & Environment","icon":"💧","title":"How Earthquakes Affect Groundwater","level":"B2","source":"U.S. Geological Survey (USGS)","sourceTitle":"Groundwater Effects from Earthquakes","sourceUrl":"https://www.usgs.gov/programs/earthquake-hazards/groundwater-effects-earthquakes","license":"USGS-authored U.S. public-domain information; Smartcom educational adaptation","paragraphs":["Groundwater fills spaces and cracks beneath the surface of Earth. During an earthquake, seismic waves travel through rock and can sometimes disturb the water stored underground. The effects may be noticed in wells, springs and other places connected to groundwater.","One possible response is a temporary rise and fall in the water level inside a well. In some cases, the level changes more permanently before gradually returning toward its earlier condition. Researchers have also observed changes in water clarity after major earthquakes, even at considerable distances from the epicenter.","These changes can happen for more than one reason. The passing seismic waves may compress and expand the surrounding rock, creating pressure variations. In other situations, an earthquake can alter underground pathways through which water flows. The exact mechanism depends on local geology and the properties of the aquifer.","Scientists use carefully recorded measurements to study these changes. This research provides information about both earthquake effects and the hidden movement of water below the ground. It also reminds us that geological events can influence natural systems beyond the places where shaking feels strongest."],"topic":"science","originalText":false},{"id":"geysers","category":"Water & Environment","icon":"♨️","title":"The Science of Geysers and Hot Springs","level":"B1–B2","source":"U.S. Geological Survey (USGS)","sourceTitle":"Volcanoes — Geysers, Fumaroles, and Hot Springs","sourceUrl":"https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/volc/text.html","license":"USGS-authored U.S. public-domain information; Smartcom educational adaptation","paragraphs":["In some volcanic regions, water beneath the ground is heated by very hot rock. This underground heat can create unusual features at the surface, including geysers, hot springs and fumaroles. Although these features may appear similar, they release heat and fluids in different ways.","A geyser is famous for its sudden eruptions of hot water and steam. Water enters underground spaces and becomes heated. Under the right conditions, pressure increases until part of the hot water turns rapidly into steam, forcing water upward. Some geysers erupt at relatively regular intervals, while others are less predictable.","Hot springs usually allow heated water to flow more steadily toward the surface. A fumarole, by contrast, releases steam and other gases rather than a large flow of liquid water. Minerals and chemical reactions may create striking colors in nearby rocks and pools.","These environments can be beautiful, but they can also be dangerous because the water and ground may be extremely hot. Scientists study them to learn about underground water systems and volcanic heat. Visitors should stay on marked paths and follow local safety instructions."],"topic":"science","originalText":false},{"id":"animals-coral","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","icon":"🐠","title":"Why Are Coral Reefs Important?","level":"B1–B2","source":"NOAA National Ocean Service","sourceTitle":"Why are coral reefs important?","sourceUrl":"https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/education/tutorial_corals/coral07_importance.html","license":"English educational adaptation of NOAA's public information (not a verbatim reproduction)","paragraphs":["Coral reefs are sometimes compared to underwater cities. Although they occupy only a small part of the ocean, they provide places where many species of fish and other marine animals can find food, shelter and space to grow. The complex shapes created by corals offer protection to smaller creatures, which may otherwise be exposed to predators.","These ecosystems also support people. Fishing communities depend on healthy marine habitats, while visitors travel to reef areas to snorkel and dive. Local businesses may benefit from tourism, and nearby communities can receive food and income from activities connected with the sea.","Reefs face several pressures. Rising ocean temperatures can disturb the relationship between corals and the tiny organisms that live inside them. Pollution and physical damage can make recovery more difficult. Because many reef species are connected through food webs, a change affecting one group may influence others.","Marine scientists study reefs to understand how these environments respond to change. Their research can inform conservation plans and help communities protect valuable coastal ecosystems."],"image":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/17693474/pexels-photo-17693474.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1200","imageCredit":"Francesco Ungaro","imageSourceUrl":"https://www.pexels.com/photo/coral-reef-and-fish-17693474/","imageLicense":"Pexels License"},{"id":"health-activity","topic":"health","category":"Health","icon":"🩺","title":"How Daily Movement Supports Health","level":"B1–B2","source":"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)","sourceTitle":"Health Benefits of Physical Activity for Adults","sourceUrl":"https://www.cdc.gov/physical-activity-basics/health-benefits/adults.html","license":"Original Smartcom educational adaptation of U.S. federal source information","paragraphs":["Physical activity does not have to mean an exhausting workout at the gym. Walking, cycling and many other forms of movement can contribute to a healthier routine. According to public health guidance, regular activity supports the heart, muscles, bones and brain.","Some benefits can appear soon after a period of exercise. A person may experience improved sleep or a temporary reduction in feelings of anxiety. Over the longer term, keeping active can help lower the risk of several chronic conditions, including heart disease and type 2 diabetes.","People have different abilities, schedules and preferences. One person may enjoy swimming, while another prefers walking in a park. Choosing activities that fit an individual's circumstances can make the habit easier to maintain. Starting with a manageable routine is often more practical than attempting a dramatic change.","Good health also depends on other factors, such as sleep, nutrition and access to medical care. Exercise is an important part of a balanced lifestyle, but it is not a substitute for professional medical advice."]},{"id":"fashion-textiles","topic":"fashion","category":"Fashion","icon":"👕","title":"What Happens to Clothes We Throw Away?","level":"B2","source":"U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)","sourceTitle":"Textiles: Material-Specific Data","sourceUrl":"https://www.epa.gov/facts-and-figures-about-materials-waste-and-recycling/textiles-material-specific-data","license":"Original Smartcom educational adaptation of U.S. federal source information","paragraphs":["Clothing is part of everyday life, but it also creates a difficult environmental question: what happens when people no longer want an item? Some clothes are sold or donated for reuse, while others enter waste systems. The final destination depends on the material, its condition and available collection services.","The United States Environmental Protection Agency studies how much textile waste is generated and how it is managed. Its estimates include clothing as well as other household textiles. The agency tracks several routes, such as recycling, energy recovery and disposal in landfills.","Recycling textiles can be challenging because garments often combine different fibres, buttons, zips and decorative materials. Sorting these components requires time and specialised equipment. Reuse is another approach. Repairing an item or giving it to someone who can wear it may extend its useful life before it becomes waste.","These choices do not solve every problem in the fashion industry, but they encourage consumers to consider durability and waste. Thinking about how clothes are made, used and discarded can change the way we shop."]},{"id":"food-safety","topic":"food","category":"Food","icon":"🥗","title":"Four Habits for Safer Food Preparation","level":"B1","source":"U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA)","sourceTitle":"How do I keep my foods safe to eat?","sourceUrl":"https://ask.fsis.usda.gov/article/How-do-I-keep-my-foods-safe-to-eat","license":"Original Smartcom educational adaptation of U.S. federal source information","paragraphs":["Preparing a meal can be enjoyable, but it also requires attention to food safety. Harmful bacteria are not always visible, and they may spread through hands, utensils or kitchen surfaces. Simple routines can help reduce the risk of illness.","One important habit is cleaning. Washing hands and keeping preparation areas clean can reduce contamination. Another is separation: raw meat and ready-to-eat foods should not share dirty cutting boards or utensils. This helps avoid moving bacteria from one item to another.","Cooking is also important. Different foods require suitable temperatures to become safe to eat, and a food thermometer can help cooks check whether food is heated sufficiently. Finally, refrigeration slows the growth of many harmful bacteria. Perishable ingredients should be chilled promptly rather than left at room temperature for long periods.","These habits are useful for beginners as well as experienced cooks. Following clear steps makes the kitchen more organised and helps families prepare meals with greater confidence."]},{"id":"travel-checklist","topic":"travel","category":"Travel","icon":"🧳","title":"Preparing for International Travel","level":"B1–B2","source":"U.S. Department of State","sourceTitle":"International Travel Checklist","sourceUrl":"https://travel.state.gov/en/international-travel/planning/checklist.html","license":"Original Smartcom educational adaptation of U.S. federal source information","paragraphs":["An international trip involves more preparation than choosing a destination. Travellers should check the entry rules of the country they plan to visit. Passport validity, visa requirements and other documents can vary widely from one place to another.","It is helpful to prepare copies of important documents. A traveller might keep electronic photographs as well as paper copies stored separately from the originals. If a passport is lost, this information may make the next steps easier. Checking official travel advice can also help visitors understand local conditions.","Transport and accommodation arrangements deserve attention too. Knowing where to stay on the first night and how to reach the location can reduce stress after a long journey. Travellers may also want to understand basic local customs and learn a few useful expressions.","Good preparation does not mean every moment must be planned. Flexibility is still valuable when schedules change. The aim is to handle essential responsibilities in advance so that more time can be spent enjoying the journey."]},{"id":"space-planets","topic":"space","category":"Space & Universe","icon":"🪐","title":"A Tour of the Eight Planets","level":"B1–B2","source":"NASA Science","sourceTitle":"About Planets","sourceUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/","license":"Original Smartcom educational adaptation of NASA public educational information","paragraphs":["Our solar system has eight planets orbiting the Sun. The four closest planets are Mercury, Venus, Earth and Mars. They are called terrestrial planets because they have solid surfaces. Their environments are very different: Mercury is small and close to the Sun, while Earth has liquid water on its surface and supports life.","Farther from the Sun are Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune. These giant planets do not have surfaces like Earth. Jupiter and Saturn are often described as gas giants, while Uranus and Neptune are known as ice giants. Each has its own atmosphere, moons and other remarkable features.","Scientists study the planets with telescopes, orbiting spacecraft and robotic missions. Images and measurements reveal storms, rings, craters and the chemical composition of distant worlds. Such observations help researchers compare how planets formed and changed over billions of years.","Beyond our solar system, astronomers have discovered thousands of exoplanets orbiting other stars. These discoveries show that planetary systems are diverse and that our neighbourhood is only one small part of a much larger universe."]},{"id":"plants-pollinators","topic":"plants","category":"Plants & Nature","icon":"🌿","title":"Why Flowers Need Pollinators","level":"B1–B2","source":"U.S. National Park Service (NPS)","sourceTitle":"About Pollinators","sourceUrl":"https://www.nps.gov/subjects/pollinators/what-is-a-pollinator.htm","license":"Original Smartcom educational adaptation of U.S. federal source information","paragraphs":["Many plants need help to reproduce. Pollination happens when pollen moves to the part of a flower where it can support the development of seeds. Wind and water can carry pollen, but insects and other animals also play an important role.","Bees often visit flowers to collect nectar and pollen. As they move between flowers, grains of pollen may stick to their bodies and be transported elsewhere. Butterflies, birds and bats can help pollinate plants in different environments. The relationship provides food for animals and supports plant reproduction.","Pollination is important beyond natural habitats. Many crops depend at least partly on pollinating animals. Without healthy populations of pollinators, producing some fruits, nuts and vegetables would become more difficult.","Pollinators face challenges that include habitat loss, certain pesticides and environmental change. Gardens with suitable flowering plants, protected natural areas and careful land management can provide valuable resources. Understanding pollination helps explain why protecting biodiversity matters to people as well as to wildlife."]},{"id":"sports-students","topic":"sports","category":"Sports","icon":"⚽","title":"Why Sports and Movement Matter at School","level":"B1–B2","source":"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)","sourceTitle":"Health Benefits of Physical Activity for Children","sourceUrl":"https://www.cdc.gov/physical-activity-basics/health-benefits/children.html","license":"Original Smartcom educational adaptation of U.S. federal source information","paragraphs":["Sport can be exciting, but its value goes beyond the score at the end of a match. Activities such as running, swimming and playing games encourage movement and help children develop physical skills. Team sports may also provide opportunities to cooperate and communicate with others.","Regular physical activity supports stronger muscles and bones. Health agencies also report benefits for attention, memory and general well-being. These advantages can make an active lifestyle valuable both inside and outside the classroom.","Students do not all enjoy the same kinds of exercise. Some prefer competitive games, while others are happier cycling, dancing or walking. Schools can encourage participation by offering different options and creating an atmosphere where beginners feel welcome.","Safe equipment, appropriate instruction and rest are important parts of any activity programme. A healthy approach to sport should allow students to learn skills, enjoy movement and develop confidence without placing unnecessary pressure on performance."]},{"topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original educational article","id":"animal-dolphin-language","icon":"🐬","title":"The Secret Conversations of Dolphins","image":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/9406885/pexels-photo-9406885.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1200","imageCredit":"Noah Munivez","imageSourceUrl":"https://www.pexels.com/photo/dolphins-swimming-in-the-ocean-9406885/","imageLicense":"Pexels License","paragraphs":["Just below the ocean's surface, a pod of dolphins moves through the water with remarkable coordination. One animal suddenly changes direction, and the others follow almost immediately. To a swimmer watching from above, the group might appear to be acting as a single creature. In fact, the dolphins are constantly exchanging information through sound and movement.","Dolphins produce whistles, clicks and other vocalisations. Clicks are especially useful for echolocation, a process that allows an animal to investigate its surroundings by listening to returning echoes. Whistles play different roles in social interactions. Researchers have found that some dolphins develop distinctive whistles that can help other members of the group recognise them.","Sound is particularly valuable underwater because visibility changes with depth, weather and the amount of material floating in the sea. A dolphin does not need to see every companion to remain in contact with the group. However, scientists are careful not to describe dolphin communication as a human language: many details about how the animals interpret particular sounds remain unknown.","Studying these interactions is not simply a matter of curiosity. Human activities, including underwater construction and shipping, introduce noise into marine habitats. Understanding how dolphins depend on sound can help researchers consider the effects of that noise on wildlife."]},{"topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original educational article","id":"animal-elephant-memory","icon":"🐘","title":"Inside the Social World of Elephants","image":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/17924665/pexels-photo-17924665.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1200","imageCredit":"Petrus Bester","imageSourceUrl":"https://www.pexels.com/photo/african-elephant-with-one-tusk-walking-through-grass-and-bushes-17924665/","imageLicense":"Pexels License","paragraphs":["An elephant family travelling across a dry landscape is rarely just a collection of animals moving in the same direction. Female elephants and their young often form close social groups, and an experienced older female may guide the herd towards water, feeding areas or safer routes. Her decisions can influence the survival of the family during difficult seasons.","Elephants gather information about their surroundings through sight, smell, touch and sound. They can produce low-frequency calls that travel farther than many of the sounds humans normally hear. Such signals may help separated animals remain in contact across a large area. Their trunks are equally remarkable: they can lift objects, investigate unfamiliar scents and offer gentle contact to another elephant.","Young calves depend on adults for protection and spend years learning how to find food and navigate their environment. Other members of the herd may respond when a calf becomes distressed. These observations have helped scientists develop a richer picture of elephant social behaviour.","Yet the animals face growing pressure where roads, farms and settlements overlap with their habitats. Conservation efforts increasingly focus on protecting connected landscapes so that elephants can continue moving between the places they need."]},{"topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original educational article","id":"animal-penguin-winter","icon":"🐧","title":"How Penguins Manage Life in the Cold","image":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/689777/pexels-photo-689777.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1200","imageCredit":"DSD","imageSourceUrl":"https://www.pexels.com/photo/penguin-on-top-of-snow-wildlife-photography-689777/","imageLicense":"Pexels License","paragraphs":["A penguin standing on Antarctic ice looks almost comically well dressed, with dark feathers on its back and pale feathers on its belly. That appearance, however, is part of a highly specialised body. Penguins have dense feathers, layers of insulating material and streamlined shapes that help them cope with harsh conditions and move efficiently through water.","Although they are birds, penguins cannot fly through the air. Instead, their powerful flippers propel them underwater, where they pursue fish, squid and small marine organisms. On land, they may walk, hop or slide across snow and ice, depending on the species and the terrain.","Penguins are not all adapted to the same climate. Some live in regions far from Antarctica, including relatively mild coastal environments. Their breeding behaviour also varies. Certain species gather in large colonies, and parents may share the work of protecting eggs and feeding their chicks.","Their lives depend closely on ocean conditions. Changes in sea ice, water temperature and food availability may affect where penguins can feed and raise young. By following penguin populations, scientists learn about the broader changes taking place in marine ecosystems."]},{"topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original educational article","id":"animal-sea-turtle","icon":"🐢","title":"The Long Journey of a Sea Turtle","image":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/7714549/pexels-photo-7714549.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1200","imageCredit":"Jake Houglum","imageSourceUrl":"https://www.pexels.com/photo/a-sea-turtle-swimming-underwater-7714549/","imageLicense":"Pexels License","paragraphs":["A young sea turtle may begin its life on a sandy beach, but the ocean soon becomes its home. After emerging from a nest, hatchlings move towards the sea and face many dangers along the way. Birds and other predators may catch them, while artificial lights near the coast can interfere with their movement towards the water.","Surviving turtles can travel enormous distances. Some species move between feeding grounds and nesting beaches separated by hundreds or even thousands of kilometres. Researchers use small tracking devices to study these journeys and understand which ocean regions the animals rely on.","Sea turtles eat different foods according to their species and age. Some feed mainly on seagrass, while others consume jellyfish, sponges or a mixture of marine organisms. Their feeding habits can influence underwater habitats and connect them to wider food webs.","Returning to a beach to reproduce is one of the most fascinating stages of a turtle's life. Scientists are still investigating the navigational cues involved. Protecting nesting sites, reducing plastic pollution and limiting accidental capture in fishing gear are important steps towards improving their chances of survival."]},{"topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original educational article","id":"animal-bee-pollination","icon":"🐝","title":"A Bee's Busy Day at the Flowers","image":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/31766091/pexels-photo-31766091.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1200","imageCredit":"Ali Goode","imageSourceUrl":"https://www.pexels.com/photo/close-up-of-a-bee-pollinating-a-flower-31766091/","imageLicense":"Pexels License","paragraphs":["On a warm morning, a bee lands on a flower and begins collecting nectar. It seems like a simple meal, but the visit may also help the plant reproduce. As the insect moves around the flower, tiny grains of pollen can become attached to its body. Some of that pollen may later reach another flower of the same species.","Pollination supports the production of seeds and fruit in many flowering plants. Bees are not the only animals that perform this task: butterflies, flies, birds and even certain bats can transfer pollen. Different flowers attract different visitors through colour, scent, shape and the availability of nectar.","Bees also have an extraordinary way of organising their work. In honeybee colonies, workers collect food, care for developing young and maintain the nest. Returning foragers can communicate information about useful food sources through movements that other bees respond to.","These insects are affected by the landscapes around them. A garden filled with a variety of flowering plants may provide food across more of the year than a lawn containing almost no flowers. Creating suitable habitats and using chemicals carefully can help support pollinators in towns and rural areas."]},{"topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original educational article","id":"animal-owl-night","icon":"🦉","title":"What Makes an Owl a Silent Hunter?","image":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/30994787/pexels-photo-30994787.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1200","imageCredit":"Saleh Bakhshiyev","imageSourceUrl":"https://www.pexels.com/photo/barn-owl-perched-on-tree-branch-at-night-30994787/","imageLicense":"Pexels License","paragraphs":["An owl crossing a dark field can be difficult to notice, even when it passes nearby. Many species are able to fly unusually quietly because of specialised feather structures that reduce the noise produced by moving air. This gives an owl an advantage when searching for small animals in the dark.","An owl's hearing is another important tool. Some species can locate prey by detecting small differences in when sounds reach their ears. Their broad facial discs help direct sound towards the ears, while their eyes are adapted for gathering light in dim conditions.","Despite popular images of owls as exclusively nocturnal creatures, their routines vary. Some hunt mostly at night, others are active around dawn and dusk, and a few can often be seen during daylight. Their preferred prey also depends on the species and habitat.","Owls are valuable members of many ecosystems because they feed on rodents and other small animals. However, changes in land use and the loss of nesting places may reduce suitable habitat. Protecting old trees and diverse landscapes can benefit owls and many other forms of wildlife."]},{"topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original educational article","id":"animal-octopus","icon":"🐙","title":"The Curious Intelligence of Octopuses","image":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/13851800/pexels-photo-13851800.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1200","imageCredit":"Ashley Christiano","imageSourceUrl":"https://www.pexels.com/photo/photo-of-an-octopus-underwater-13851800/","imageLicense":"Pexels License","paragraphs":["With eight flexible arms and a soft body, an octopus looks unlike most animals people encounter. It can squeeze through surprisingly narrow openings, move across the seabed and change its appearance using specialised cells in its skin. These abilities make the octopus a remarkable animal to observe.","Researchers have become especially interested in octopus problem-solving. In experiments and carefully designed observations, some octopuses have learned to open containers, explore unfamiliar objects and find ways around obstacles. Their behaviour suggests a sophisticated ability to interact with the surrounding environment.","An octopus also has an unusual nervous system. A large number of its nerve cells are distributed through its arms, allowing complex local control of movement and touch. Scientists continue to investigate how this organisation relates to perception and learning.","Most octopuses live comparatively short lives, and their behaviour varies widely among species. Many are solitary for much of their adult lives. Protecting coastal habitats from pollution and damage is important because these animals depend on healthy marine environments."]},{"topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original educational article","id":"animal-giraffe","icon":"🦒","title":"Why Giraffes Have Such Unusual Bodies","image":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/13915034/pexels-photo-13915034.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1200","imageCredit":"Creative Broccoli","imageSourceUrl":"https://www.pexels.com/photo/photograph-of-a-giraffe-eating-leaves-13915034/","imageLicense":"Pexels License","paragraphs":["A giraffe feeding among the branches of an acacia tree is one of the most recognisable scenes on an African savanna. Its extraordinary height allows it to reach leaves beyond the range of many other grazing animals. Yet a tall body also creates challenges, particularly when the animal bends down to drink.","Giraffes have long necks, but they possess the same number of neck vertebrae as most mammals. Their individual vertebrae are simply much longer. Powerful muscles help support the neck, while a specialised circulatory system maintains blood flow despite the distance between the heart and head.","The animals use long, mobile tongues and flexible lips to grasp leaves. Their preferred food changes with the season and the plants available. Giraffes also spend time resting, moving between feeding areas and interacting with members of their social groups.","Today, the greatest threats to many giraffe populations come from habitat changes, fragmentation and human activity. Studying their movements can help conservationists identify safe routes between areas where food and water remain available."]},{"topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original educational article","id":"animal-wolf-pack","icon":"🐺","title":"The Reality of Life in a Wolf Pack","image":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/17849313/pexels-photo-17849313.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1200","imageCredit":"Ana Pimentel","imageSourceUrl":"https://www.pexels.com/photo/wolf-in-forest-17849313/","imageLicense":"Pexels License","paragraphs":["The distant howl of a wolf can sound mysterious, but for the animals themselves it serves practical social purposes. Wolves use vocalisations, scent marks and body language to communicate with other members of their group. A howl may help separated individuals remain in contact or signal the presence of a pack in an area.","Wild wolf packs are often family groups that include a breeding pair and their offspring from different years. Older animals may help younger pack members gain experience, while group hunting can be useful when pursuing large prey. Nevertheless, the size and behaviour of a pack depend on local conditions and food availability.","Wolves influence ecosystems in several ways. As predators, they affect the animals they hunt, and scavengers may feed on remains left behind. The relationship between wolves, prey and vegetation is complex, however, and rarely has a single simple explanation.","Because wolves range over large territories, they sometimes come into conflict with livestock owners and rural communities. Conservation programmes therefore need to consider both ecological research and the concerns of people living near wolf habitats."]},{"topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original educational article","id":"animal-reef","icon":"🐠","title":"A Neighbourhood Beneath the Coral Reef","image":"https://images.pexels.com/photos/10141408/pexels-photo-10141408.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1200","imageCredit":"Saad Alaiyadhi","imageSourceUrl":"https://www.pexels.com/photo/coral-reef-and-fish-10141408/","imageLicense":"Pexels License","paragraphs":["A coral reef can be crowded with life even when the surrounding water appears almost empty. Small fish shelter among branching corals, while larger animals patrol open channels. Every crevice can offer a place to hide, feed or rest, creating a complex habitat that resembles a busy neighbourhood.","Corals are animals rather than plants. Many reef-building corals live with microscopic algae that use sunlight to produce energy. This relationship helps support growth in clear, shallow waters. The hard skeletons produced by colonies gradually create the structures on which other organisms depend.","Some reef fish eat algae, others feed on tiny animals, and certain species clean parasites from their neighbours. The interactions can be highly specialised. A change to one part of this network may alter conditions for many other species.","Reefs are vulnerable to warming seas, poor water quality and physical damage. When corals lose the algae living inside them, they can become pale in a process known as bleaching. Not every bleached coral dies, but prolonged stress can make recovery difficult. Conservation requires both local protection and broader efforts to address environmental change."]},{"id":"red-fox","title":"Red Foxes: The Art of Adapting","icon":"🦊","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"red fox","subCategory":"Foxes","paragraphs":["A red fox pauses beside a hedgerow, listening for movement beneath the grass. Its pointed ears can detect the faint sounds of small mammals, while its bushy tail helps with balance and warmth. Although often associated with woodlands, red foxes also live near farms, villages and busy cities.","Their ability to adapt is partly explained by an exceptionally varied diet. Depending on the season, foxes may eat rodents, birds, insects, fruit or food discarded by humans. This flexibility allows them to survive where more specialised predators struggle. In urban areas they often become most active after dark, when streets are quieter.","Foxes are usually solitary hunters, but adults may share the responsibility of raising young. Cubs spend their early weeks in a den before gradually exploring the surrounding area. Their future depends on access to safe shelter and enough food. Watching foxes from a distance offers a reminder that wildlife continues to live alongside people, even in heavily developed places."]},{"id":"snow-leopard","title":"Snow Leopards on the Roof of the World","icon":"🐆","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"snow leopard","subCategory":"Wild Cats","paragraphs":["High in the mountains of Central Asia, a snow leopard moves across a narrow rocky ledge. Its thick fur provides insulation against freezing temperatures, while wide paws help it travel across snow. A long tail serves as a counterweight on steep slopes and can be wrapped around the body for extra warmth.","Snow leopards are elusive animals. Their pale coats blend into the mountains, making them difficult to observe. They mainly hunt wild sheep and goats, but may also take smaller prey. Because these cats occupy enormous territories, researchers use camera traps and other non-invasive methods to study their movements.","The future of snow leopards is tied to the lives of local communities. As grazing animals move into mountain habitats, conflicts can arise when predators attack livestock. Conservation programmes work with herders to improve protection for domestic animals while preserving the natural prey and landscapes that snow leopards need."]},{"id":"orangutan-forest","title":"Orangutans and the Fragile Forest Canopy","icon":"🦧","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"orangutan","subCategory":"Primates","paragraphs":["An orangutan travels through the rainforest by reaching slowly from branch to branch. Its long arms and powerful hands are well suited to life in the trees. Unlike many social primates, adult orangutans often spend much of their time alone, although mothers maintain close relationships with their young.","Young orangutans learn by observing their mothers. They must recognise edible plants, choose safe routes and sometimes use simple tools. This learning takes years, which is one reason orangutans reproduce slowly. A female typically invests a great deal of time in raising each infant.","The forests of Borneo and Sumatra support orangutans as well as countless other species. When forests are fragmented by roads, fires or land conversion, animals may become isolated from food and breeding areas. Protecting large, connected areas of rainforest is therefore important not only for orangutans but also for the health of the wider ecosystem."]},{"id":"cheetah-speed","title":"Built for Speed: The Cheetah's Special Design","icon":"🐆","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"cheetah","subCategory":"Wild Cats","paragraphs":["On an open grassland, a cheetah watches a herd of antelope before accelerating into a short, astonishing sprint. Its flexible spine, long legs and relatively light body allow rapid changes in stride length. A long tail helps the animal maintain balance as it turns sharply while following prey.","Speed, however, comes at a cost. A cheetah cannot maintain its fastest pace for long, so it usually approaches prey carefully before giving chase. Successful hunts depend on timing, distance and the surrounding terrain. After running, the animal may need time to recover before eating.","Cheetahs face pressure from declining habitat and conflict with people who keep livestock. They also need enough space to move between areas where prey is available. Conservationists study their movements and work with communities to reduce conflict. The cheetah's story shows how a remarkable physical adaptation can still leave a species vulnerable to environmental change."]},{"id":"humpback-whale","title":"Why Humpback Whales Sing","icon":"🐋","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"humpback whale","subCategory":"Marine Life","paragraphs":["A humpback whale rises from the sea and releases a cloud of mist into the air. Beneath the surface, some males produce long sequences of sounds that researchers describe as songs. The patterns may continue for extended periods and can change gradually over a breeding season.","Scientists study these songs with underwater microphones and recordings collected across different locations. Groups of whales in the same region may share similar patterns, and changes can spread between populations. Researchers are still exploring why these sounds are produced and what they communicate in different circumstances.","Humpback whales also use a wide range of other sounds and behaviours. They migrate between feeding and breeding areas and may cooperate while hunting small fish. Their environment is increasingly affected by shipping noise, fishing gear and changing ocean conditions. Understanding whale communication helps scientists think more carefully about protecting marine animals in a noisy world."]},{"id":"polar-bear-sea-ice","title":"Polar Bears and a Changing Arctic","icon":"🐻‍❄️","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"polar bear","subCategory":"Arctic Wildlife","paragraphs":["A polar bear walks across a stretch of sea ice, occasionally lifting its head to test the air. The Arctic predator relies heavily on the ice as a platform for hunting seals. Its broad feet distribute weight and provide traction, while thick fur and a layer of fat help conserve heat.","Polar bears are strong swimmers, but travelling through open water generally requires more energy than walking on ice. When sea ice forms later or melts earlier, some bears must change where and how they search for food. The impact differs between regions, depending on local geography and conditions.","Researchers monitor polar bears through field observations, remote tracking and studies of their habitats. Their work provides information about how changes in the Arctic affect animals at the top of the food web. Protecting polar bears ultimately involves understanding the wider environment on which their survival depends."]},{"id":"red-panda-trees","title":"The Red Panda's Life Among the Branches","icon":"🐾","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"red panda","subCategory":"Forest Mammals","paragraphs":["A red panda rests along a tree branch, its long striped tail hanging beneath it. Despite its familiar name, the species is not a close relative of the giant panda. It belongs to its own distinctive family and lives in temperate mountain forests across parts of the Himalayas and nearby regions.","Red pandas spend considerable time in trees, where they can rest and avoid some dangers. They mainly eat bamboo leaves, although their diet may include other plant material. Because bamboo provides relatively little energy, the animals often conserve energy through long periods of rest.","Forest fragmentation threatens red pandas by separating habitats and making movement more difficult. Local conservation groups work to protect forests and reduce disturbance. For visitors, the animal's appealing appearance may draw attention, but the broader story is about preserving the complex mountain ecosystems that support it."]},{"id":"meerkat-watch","title":"Meerkats: The Lookouts of the Desert","icon":"🐾","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"meerkat","subCategory":"Small Mammals","paragraphs":["In the dry landscapes of southern Africa, a meerkat stands upright on its hind legs while other members of the group search for food. From this position it can scan the area for predators. If danger approaches, alarm calls can alert companions to take cover.","Meerkats live in cooperative social groups. Adults may help guard young animals, maintain burrows and search for insects and other small prey. Roles can change throughout the day. The group's success depends on communication and on an ability to respond quickly to changing conditions.","Their burrows provide shelter from heat and predators, while their social behaviour allows them to exploit challenging habitats. Although meerkats are sometimes portrayed as comic characters in popular media, their cooperation involves complex decisions. Observing them reveals how teamwork can help small animals survive in a demanding environment."]},{"id":"chameleon-colour","title":"The Real Reason Chameleons Change Colour","icon":"🦎","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"chameleon","subCategory":"Reptiles","paragraphs":["A chameleon waits quietly on a branch, gripping the wood with specialised feet. Its eyes can move in different directions, helping it watch for insects and potential danger. When prey comes close enough, the animal can extend its tongue rapidly to capture it.","Chameleons are famous for changing colour, but the process is more complicated than simply matching a background. Colour changes can be influenced by light, temperature, stress and communication with other animals. Certain species can display striking patterns during social encounters.","Many chameleons depend on specific habitats and are sensitive to environmental disturbance. Because species vary greatly in size, colour and behaviour, researchers must study them in their natural settings. Their extraordinary features show how evolution can produce unexpected solutions to the challenges of hunting, movement and communication."]},{"id":"seahorse-fathers","title":"Seahorse Fathers and Their Unusual Role","icon":"🐴","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"seahorse","subCategory":"Marine Life","paragraphs":["A seahorse drifts among seagrass, holding onto a plant stem with its curled tail. Its upright posture and horse-like head make it instantly recognisable, yet seahorses are fish. They swim using a small fin on their back and rely on camouflage to avoid being noticed.","One of their most remarkable features concerns reproduction. In many seahorse species, the female transfers eggs into a pouch on the male's body. The male carries the developing young and later releases them into the water. This arrangement is unusual among familiar fish species.","Seahorses often live in shallow coastal habitats such as seagrass beds, mangroves and coral reefs. These areas can be damaged by pollution, coastal construction and destructive fishing practices. Protecting suitable habitats is essential for the survival of these delicate animals and the many other species that share their surroundings."]},{"id":"honey-badger","title":"The Tough Life of a Honey Badger","icon":"🦡","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"honey badger","subCategory":"Small Mammals","paragraphs":["The honey badger has developed a reputation for boldness, but its survival depends on far more than courage. This stocky mammal possesses strong claws, a powerful sense of smell and loose, tough skin. These features help it dig for food and defend itself in difficult encounters.","Honey badgers are opportunistic feeders. Their diet can include insects, small vertebrates, eggs and plant material. They may raid beehives for larvae and honey, which helps explain their name. Despite dramatic stories shared online, much of their everyday life involves ordinary searching and careful use of available resources.","The species lives across parts of Africa and Asia in a variety of environments. Its ability to use different food sources can be an advantage, but it may also bring the animal into conflict with farmers and beekeepers. Accurate observation is important because exaggerated tales can hide the real ecological role of an unusual predator."]},{"id":"eagle-eyes","title":"How Eagles See the World","icon":"🦅","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"eagle","subCategory":"Birds","paragraphs":["An eagle circles high above a valley with its wings stretched wide. From that height, it can survey a large area without using much energy. Its broad wings take advantage of rising air currents, allowing the bird to soar for long periods.","Eagles are known for excellent vision. Their eyes can detect details at distances that would be difficult for humans, helping them locate potential prey. Different species have different hunting strategies: some catch fish near the water, while others pursue mammals, birds or reptiles. The shape of the wings and talons reflects these demands.","These birds often require large territories and suitable nesting places. Changes in habitat, accidental poisoning and collisions with human structures can threaten some populations. Monitoring eagle nests and movements can help conservationists identify risks. Their presence also offers a powerful illustration of how predators are connected to the health of wider ecosystems."]},{"id":"axolotl-regeneration","title":"The Axolotl and Its Remarkable Ability to Regrow","icon":"🦎","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"axolotl","subCategory":"Amphibians","paragraphs":["An axolotl looks like a creature from an illustrated fantasy book, with delicate external gills fanning out beside its head. It is actually a salamander native to the lakes and canals around Mexico City. Unlike many amphibians, it typically retains aquatic features throughout its adult life.","Axolotls attract scientific interest because they can regenerate certain damaged tissues, including parts of limbs. Researchers study the biological processes involved, hoping to learn more about how cells communicate during repair. This does not mean that humans will soon be able to regrow limbs; translating discoveries between species is a difficult task.","In the wild, axolotls face serious pressures from habitat loss, poor water quality and introduced animals. Their popularity in laboratories and aquariums contrasts sharply with their threatened status in native habitats. Protecting the wetlands around their remaining populations is essential if future generations are to encounter these animals beyond captivity."]},{"id":"capybara-river","title":"Why Capybaras Love the Water","icon":"🐹","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"capybara","subCategory":"South American Wildlife","paragraphs":["A capybara sits calmly beside a river while birds move through the grass nearby. The largest living rodent is native to South America and is closely associated with wetlands, lakes and slow-moving waterways. Its partially webbed feet make swimming easier, and it can quickly enter the water when danger approaches.","Capybaras usually feed on grasses and other plants. They often live in social groups, particularly where food and water are plentiful. Their communication includes whistles, barks and other sounds that help group members respond to different situations.","Water provides more than a place to escape predators. It can also help capybaras cool down during hot weather. Although these animals have become popular in internet videos, wild populations depend on functioning wetlands and landscapes that support many species. Understanding their natural habits is more informative than treating them simply as unusually relaxed pets."]},{"id":"flamingo-pink","title":"Where Flamingos Get Their Pink Feathers","icon":"🦩","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"flamingo","subCategory":"Wetland Birds","paragraphs":["A flock of flamingos stands in shallow water, its bright pink feathers creating an unforgettable scene. The colour is linked to pigments called carotenoids, which the birds obtain through their food. Young flamingos are generally much duller in colour, and their feathers become more colourful as they mature.","Flamingos use specialised bills to filter small organisms and particles from water. Their preferred food differs among species and habitats, but may include tiny crustaceans and algae. Many flamingos gather in enormous colonies, where nesting and feeding depend on suitable water conditions.","Wetlands are constantly changing environments. Drought, pollution and human disturbance can affect the food supplies and nesting places flamingos need. By monitoring breeding colonies, researchers gain information about the health of lakes and coastal wetlands. The birds' striking appearance is therefore connected to a wider environmental story."]},{"id":"beaver-engineers","title":"Beavers: Nature's River Engineers","icon":"🦫","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"beaver","subCategory":"Freshwater Wildlife","paragraphs":["A beaver carries a branch through a stream and adds it to a growing structure of sticks and mud. By building dams, beavers can slow water and create ponds. These changes may increase wetland habitat for some species while affecting the flow of streams and local vegetation.","Beavers have strong front teeth that continue growing throughout their lives. They use them to cut small trees and branches, which become food or building materials. Their broad tails are useful in the water and can also be slapped against the surface as a warning signal.","Because beaver activity can change drainage patterns, people sometimes regard the animals as pests, especially where flooding affects roads or farmland. In other places, their ponds are valued for supporting biodiversity and storing water. Wildlife managers study local conditions to decide how people and beavers can share the same landscape."]},{"id":"tree-frog","title":"Tree Frogs and the Art of Climbing","icon":"🐸","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"tree frog","subCategory":"Amphibians","paragraphs":["A small tree frog clings to the smooth surface of a leaf after a tropical rainstorm. Many species possess specialised toe pads that provide grip, allowing them to move across vegetation and other surfaces. Their lightweight bodies help them reach places that larger animals cannot easily access.","Frogs use calls for several reasons, including attracting mates and communicating during the breeding season. A pond at night may be filled with overlapping sounds from different species. These calls can help researchers identify animals that are difficult to see among dense plants.","Amphibians have sensitive skin and often depend on moist habitats. Changes in rainfall, pollution and disease can affect their survival. Because frogs respond quickly to environmental conditions, scientists sometimes study them as indicators of ecosystem health. Protecting wetlands and forests helps preserve the remarkable variety of tree frogs around the world."]},{"id":"great-white-shark","title":"Great White Sharks Beyond the Stereotype","icon":"🦈","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"great white shark","subCategory":"Marine Predators","paragraphs":["A large shark moves silently through the water, its body held steady by powerful muscles and a streamlined shape. Great white sharks are widely portrayed as aggressive monsters, but their behaviour is much more complex. They are predators that respond to food availability, environmental conditions and opportunities to hunt.","Young great white sharks may eat fish and other smaller animals. As they grow, some individuals also hunt marine mammals. Their senses help them detect movement, chemical traces and electrical signals produced by living organisms. Researchers track sharks to understand seasonal movements and important habitats.","Human encounters with great white sharks attract considerable attention, although serious incidents are rare compared with the number of people entering the ocean. Conservation efforts focus on reducing accidental capture and protecting marine ecosystems. Understanding the species through careful research provides a more balanced picture than sensational headlines."]},{"id":"raven-intelligence","title":"Ravens: The Problem-Solvers of the Bird World","icon":"🐦‍⬛","topic":"animals","category":"Animals","level":"B1–B2","source":"","sourceTitle":"","sourceUrl":"","license":"Original English-language educational article","photoSearch":"raven","subCategory":"Bird Intelligence","paragraphs":["A raven lands beside a roadside object and examines it carefully before moving on. Ravens are members of the crow family and have attracted attention for their flexible behaviour. They can learn from experience, recognise patterns and investigate unfamiliar situations.","Some studies have explored how ravens interact with objects or cooperate with other birds. The results suggest that these animals can solve certain problems in ways that go beyond simple trial and error. However, researchers are careful about attributing human motives to bird behaviour. An impressive task in a laboratory does not mean that a raven thinks exactly as a person does.","Ravens live in a wide range of habitats, including mountains, forests and urban areas. Their varied diet allows them to exploit different opportunities, from insects and fruit to animal remains. As scavengers and predators, they contribute to the movement of nutrients through ecosystems. Their adaptability makes them an especially interesting species for studying animal cognition."]}];
